  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1135 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nThe pattern remained primarily unchanged over the past few days\nwith a squat mid-level ridge spinning over the Dessert \nSouthwest and quasi-zonal flow stretching from Montana through \nthe Great Lakes. Monsoonal moisture is evident turning through \nthe Southwest and continuing east into Nebraska. \n\nAt 10pm tonight, we're watching some glaciation in northeast\nNebraska due in part to warm air advection from the south and a\nweak shortwave passing through. These updrafts are likely to \nstruggle with the bulk of the moisture rooted below the \ninversion at about 750 hPa. Better chances of deep convection \nwait for the complexes that have developed west of here to pass \nthrough the area. \n\nThere remains some uncertainty with the eventual evolution and\npath of the complex working its way diagonally across South \nDakota, following a theta-e gradient. The HRRR has been wavering\non whether to bring it far enough south to affect the OAX CWA,\nbut solutions that do bring it this way seem to be coming at a \nslower pace than earlier. Other 00z convection allowing models \nare more consistently farther south, which would clip the CWA \nwith severe criteria wind. The last few HRRR runs have been \nkeeping the SD MCC well north of Sioux City and Nebraska, but \nhave allowed the dying convection west of here to be \nreinvigorated over the northeastern portion of the state as the \nMCC passes 75 miles north of it. These individual cells should \nbe monitored for severe gusts or a brief period of large hail, \nbut severe weather has less than a 10% probability from these \npossible cells. I'd put the chance of a warning needing to be \nissued from the northern complex at about 15-20% as of 11pm. \nShould it end up farther south than currently progged, damaging \nwind would be the primary threat.",
